    <Template />
  </Info>
  <Properties>
    <description>
      <Value><![CDATA[The ground is rapidly shifting under everyone as AI reshapes what leadership looks like in the tech industry. Engineering managers are tasked with leading teams through complex new Machine Learning and Generative AI paradigms, but there is a hidden cost to this rapid innovation: a massive spike in imposter syndrome. Feeling like a fraud is a widespread psychological experience of self-doubt, and when technical teams face uncharted territory, this fear of being "found out" can paralyze progress.

Drawing on 15 years tech experience, as well as leading global teams and AI/ML initiatives at a multi-billion-dollar SaaS company, this workshop merges technical leadership with practical psychological frameworks. We will reframe imposter syndrome not as a lack of ability, but as a powerful signal that you and your team are at the edge of your comfort zone, actively stretching into new, innovative territory.

Key Takeaways:
Navigating the "Valley of Despair": Attendees will learn how to use the Dunning-Kruger effect to help their engineers understand that a sudden drop in confidence when learning complex new AI skills is a natural step in the learning process, not a sign of failure.
The Milestones Audit for Tech Teams: A hands-on exercise where leaders practice building self-trust by auditing past hard-earned technical achievements (like learning new architectures, shipping difficult projects, or launching products) to ground their confidence in reality instead of waiting to "feel ready".
The 10% Thought Shift for Leaders: A practical cognitive framework to combat the fear of being "found out." Instead of attempting to leap from "I have no idea what I'm doing" to complete expert confidence, leaders will practice shifting their self-doubting thoughts to be just 10% more believable and supportive, enabling them to lead boldly through uncertainty.]]></Value>
    </description>
